course: everyday
lesson: 374
title: What fights cost: bruises, noses and teeth
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: For weeks Maddie had been shoving boys in her class. Then she shoved Connor, who always says he is «all for equality». Connor said «you want to fight like the boys, you get hit like one» and punched her on the nose. For a second he felt proud; then he saw the blood and felt sick. Maddie's nose bled all over her shirt and needed an X-ray (bruised, not broken). Connor was excluded (sent home from school) for two days, Maddie got a detention for the shove, and both sets of parents were called in. Equality means nobody hits anybody, not that everybody may hit everybody.
explain: The nose cost more the next week: the swelling spread and Maddie had two black eyes for the school photo. The photo ended up on Gran's mantelpiece, and every time Maddie saw it she remembered that the shove came first.
explain: Jake pushed a boy over during a scrap, and the boy hit his head on the pavement and went very quiet. It was the worst fear Jake had ever felt. Someone called 999, an ambulance came, and the boy had a concussion; he was fine after a week, but Jake could not sleep for nights. One push can do far more harm than anyone means.
explain: The list of costs goes on. Jake got a black eye that swelled shut for two days and missed the class football final, where he would have been in goal. A boy Jake was pinning down bit him, and the bite got infected. Maddie punched a boy's arm, sprained her fingers and could not hold a pen for the spelling test; Lily helped her kindly, without saying «I told you so». Another time a bigger boy pushed Maddie off a wall after she called him names, and she fractured (cracked) her wrist. Her mum said «he shouldn't have pushed you» and «you started it» were both true, and neither made it OK.
explain: Real fights are messy and ugly, not like on TV. Jake chipped his front tooth over a football card that was not even rare, and the dentist cost his parents money. Kieran split his chin on the kerb and needed four stitches. Sam was not even fighting, just watching, and a flying elbow gave him a fat lip for his cousin's wedding photos.
fact: A punch to the nose often causes bleeding and swelling that can spread into two black eyes.
fact: A head that hits the ground can cause a concussion, an injury to the brain that needs a doctor.
fact: If someone hits their head and goes quiet, sleepy or confused, call 999 or 911 and get an adult straight away.
fact: Adult teeth do not grow back; a chipped tooth needs a dentist to repair it.
fact: Pavements, kerbs and walls are hard, so falls during a fight cause cuts, stitches and broken bones.
fact: The small bones in the fingers and hand break or sprain easily when they punch something.
fact: Human bites easily get infected and often need antibiotics (medicine that kills germs).
fact: People watching a fight can get hurt by accident too.
fact: Saying who «started it» does not make anyone's injury hurt less or heal faster.
fact: Nobody deserves to be hurt, and starting trouble with words or shoves still has a cost.
exercise: What had Maddie been doing for weeks before Connor punched her? | Shoving boys in her class | Helping boys with homework | Ignoring every boy | Telling the teacher about boys
exercise: How did Connor feel just after the punch? | Proud for a second, then sick when he saw the blood | Proud all day long | Sorry before he even hit her, so he held back the punch | He did not notice anything
exercise: What did the X-ray of Maddie's nose show? | It was bruised, not broken | It was broken in three places | There was nothing wrong at all | Her tooth was chipped
exercise: Connor said he was «all for equality». What does equality really mean here? | Nobody hits anybody | Everybody may hit everybody | Girls may hit but boys may not | Boys may hit but girls may not
exercise: What happened to each of them at school? | Connor was excluded for two days; Maddie got a detention for the shove | Only Maddie was punished, because she had shoved Connor first | Only Connor was punished | Nobody was punished, as it was a fair fight
exercise: Why did Maddie have two black eyes for the school photo? | The swelling from her nose spread to both eyes | She was hit twice more that week | She had a cold | She had been wearing dark make-up to look tough
exercise: What did Maddie think every time she saw the photo on Gran's mantelpiece? | That the shove came first | That she looked tough and brilliant | That Connor should be in it too | Nothing, she never looked at it
exercise: Jake got a black eye that swelled shut for two days. What did it cost him? | Missing the class football final, where he would have been in goal | A day off school watching TV | Nothing at all, because the black eye healed overnight | A new pair of glasses
exercise: Jake chipped his front tooth fighting about a football card. What is true about the tooth? | It will not grow back and needed the dentist | It will grow back on its own in a few weeks, like a nail | It is not important at all | Only baby teeth can chip
exercise: The boy Jake pushed hit his head on the pavement and went very quiet. What was the right thing to do? | Call 999 and get an adult straight away | Help him up and send him home on his own to have a lie-down | Wait an hour to see if he wakes up | Run off before anyone sees
exercise: Maddie punched a boy's arm and sprained her fingers. What did it cost her? | She could not hold a pen for the spelling test | Nothing happened to her, because the boy's arm was soft | She won a prize for strength | She got a day off school
exercise: How should Lily help when Maddie can't hold her pen? | Kindly, without saying «I told you so» | By laughing at her fingers | By doing badly in her own spelling test too, to keep her company | By telling everyone what happened
exercise: Jake got a bite mark from a boy he was pinning down. Why did he need antibiotics? | The bite got infected | Bites are always poisonous | He was already ill | The nurse gives them for every injury
exercise: Sam was only watching a fight. What happened to him? | A flying elbow gave him a fat lip | Nothing, watchers are always safe | He won the fight by accident | He was given a medal for watching
exercise: A bigger boy pushed Maddie off a wall after she called him names. She fractured her wrist. What did Maddie's mum say? | «He shouldn't have pushed you» and «you started it» were both true, and neither made it OK | Maddie deserved it, because she was the one calling names in the first place | The boy did nothing wrong at all | It was all the wall's fault
exercise: Which lesson fits all the injuries in this story best? | Fights cost far more than anyone expects, even to people watching | Only the loser of a fight ever gets hurt; the winner walks away fine | Small fights never cause real injuries | Injuries are a sign you fought well
test: «he banged his head and he's just lying there quiet, he's fine yeah?» Best move? | No, call 999 and get an adult now | Yes, let him sleep it off on the bench | Splash water and leave | Walk him home on your own
test: «Connor said she wanted equality so she got hit, fair's fair». Is it? | No, equality means nobody should hit anyone | Yes, that is exactly what equality means in the playground | Yes, because she is a tomboy | Only if she hit first with a fist
test: «Maddie looks well hard in that photo with two shiners, haha». What's the wiser view? | Her injury is a reminder of the fight, not a trophy | She should get more black eyes for the next school photo | It is the best photo of the year | Black eyes make you look grown-up
test: «gonna scrap him for that card, it's MINE». Jake did that once. What did it really cost? | A chipped tooth and a dentist's bill, for a common card | Nothing, because he got the card back in the end | A rare card for free | A day off school
test: «there's a fight round the back, come watch!» What's the safe choice? | Walk away from it and tell an adult | Push to the front to see better | Film it from close up | Cheer for your mate so he knows you're on his side
test: Kieran's chin's split and bleeding after a scuffle on the kerb. What's right? | Get an adult, he may need stitches | Tell him to rub some dirt on it and carry on playing | Carry on with the scuffle | Take a photo for the group chat
test: «just punch his arm, it's soft, won't hurt you». What does Maddie know now? | Your fingers can sprain or break doing that | Arms are too soft to hurt your hand, so it's fine | It only hurts the other person | Punching an arm is always safe
test: «he bit me in the fight, it's just a scratch». Two days later it's red and puffy. Why tell an adult? | Bites often get infected and need treatment | Red means it is healing nicely | Bites never get infected | So the other boy gets told off by his mum for biting
test: «she called him names so she deserved the wall, innit». What's true? | Nobody deserves to be hurt; name-calling still has a cost too | She deserved to break her wrist | Name-calling is worse than pushing, so she got what she asked for | It was nobody's fault at all
test: Two kids are arguing on top of a high wall in the park, one about to shove. What's the biggest danger? | A fall onto the hard ground, causing broken bones or a head injury | Getting their clothes muddy and having to explain it at home | Missing their tea | The park keeper hearing them
test: Connor felt proud for a second, then sick when he saw blood. What does that suggest about fights? | Many people don't really want the harm a fight does once they see it | Hitting always feels good afterwards, once the shock wears off | Blood is the best part of a fight | Connor was ill already that day
exam: Identify the principal action required where a person has struck their head upon the ground and subsequently become unusually quiet. | The emergency services ought to be summoned and an adult informed without delay | The person ought to be left to rest undisturbed | The person ought to be escorted home unaccompanied by another pupil in order to rest | No action is required unless bleeding is observed
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principle of equality in respect of physical violence? | Equality requires that no person strike any other person | Equality permits any person whatsoever to strike any other person | Equality permits retaliation by either sex | Equality has no bearing upon physical violence
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning an adult tooth fractured in a fight. | It does not regenerate and requires repair by a dentist | It regenerates within several weeks | It requires no treatment of any kind, the chip being of a cosmetic nature only | It is replaced by a milk tooth
exam: It is given that a bystander, not a participant, observed a fight at close quarters. Ascertain the risk to which the bystander was exposed. | Accidental injury from the movements of the participants | No risk, bystanders being invariably safe | A risk of punishment as the principal aggressor, even where no blow is struck | A risk solely of boredom
